export default {
  "overview": "# Where to start: three development paths\n\n## Three paths\n\n\"Development\" on dsh-ee splits into three paths by deliverable: writing DSH plugins, writing dsh-ee extensions, and connecting non-DSH clients. They share the same company identity and tokens, but differ in code, checks and publishing entries.\n\n| Path | What you deliver | Where it lands in dsh-ee | Starting tool |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| DSH plugin author | A DSH plugin package (tools, clients, services, commands, etc.) | Company npm registry, publish precheck, review, distribution | First `dsh plugin add dsh-primer`, then scaffold with `dsh-primer new` and self-check with `dsh-primer check` |\n| dsh-ee extension author | An extension: declarative / service / local (built-in ones are implemented by dsh-ee itself) | Connections, fields & access rules, Agent skills | Scaffold with `dsh-ee extension init`, self-check with `dsh-ee extension check` |\n| Non-DSH client | A client that calls models and MCP over HTTP | My tokens, model gateway, `/v1`, `/mcp` and the discovery endpoint | Create a token in \"My tokens\" in the Workbench first; see [Connect a third-party Harness](/docs/harness-quickstart) |\n\nAn extension comes in four forms (declarative / service / local / built-in), and the manifest can only declare the first three `kind` values; see [Write a dsh-ee extension](/docs/extension-quickstart) for details.\n\n## First, tell plugins and extensions apart\n\nA **DSH plugin** is installed in the DSH client and built and verified by its author on their own machine; dsh-ee handles company-side admission, hosting and distribution.\n\nA **dsh-ee extension** is the integration unit that connects to the dsh-ee server. It is managed by dsh-ee's own CLI, and administrators configure it in \"Connections\" and \"Fields & access rules\".\n\n| Aspect | DSH plugin | dsh-ee extension |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Where it runs | The machine running the DSH client | Requests from inside the dsh-ee process (declarative), the company's own service (service), employee machines (local) |\n| Manifest file | `package.json` and `cordis.patch.yml` | `dsh-ee.extension.json` |\n| Publishing entry | The company npm registry, or \"Share a plugin\" in the Workbench | Upload a zip for declarative; administrators fill in the service address for service extensions; deliver an npm package plus manifest for local |\n| Who can install | Employees with the \"Install capabilities\" permission | Configured by administrators in connections and rules |\n\n## Suggested reading order\n\n1. Publishing a plugin for the first time: [Write a DSH plugin and publish it to the company registry](/docs/plugin-quickstart)\n2. Writing an extension for the first time: [Write a dsh-ee extension: manifest, checks and calls](/docs/extension-quickstart)\n3. Connecting a third-party Harness or your own client: [Connect a third-party Harness or your own client](/docs/harness-quickstart)\n4. Confirming permission boundaries: [Permissions and access rules: a quick reference](/docs/access-rules)\n5. Wanting to know what you will be asked before submitting: [Publish entries and how to read the precheck](/docs/publish-precheck)\n6. Working out where standards and checks meet: [Standards and checks: ENTRY / FLOW / BOOST](/docs/quality-standards)\n\n## What else to prepare\n\n| Item | Notes |\n| --- | --- |\n| Account and permissions | Publishing plugins needs the \"Publish capabilities\" permission (`registry.publish`); extension development materials need \"Develop extensions\" (`extensions.develop`) |\n| Company registry address | Get the npm registry address, token configuration and `setup.sh` usage from the \"Setup guide\" |\n| Local toolchain | Node ≥24.9 (the `engines` requirement of `dsh-primer` and `dsh-standards`) and pnpm; Go 1.27+ only for service extensions |\n| Network | On an intranet deployment, GitHub and the public npm may be unreachable; confirm dependencies can be fetched first, and use \"upstream preload\" when necessary |\n\n## Site guides and repository docs\n\nThe site pages only give dsh-ee-side summaries and entry points and do not copy upstream text; repository docs may be quoted or adapted under their respective licenses (CC BY 4.0 requires keeping TASL and a Change Notice; Apache-2.0 requires keeping LICENSE/NOTICE). For the text, licenses and upstream versions, the repository docs and upstream repositories are authoritative (links point to the docs after merge):\n\n| Content | Where |\n| --- | --- |\n| Development docs index (quickstart / extensions / Harness / publishing / standards / errors & FAQ) | [docs/dev/README.md (available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/dev/README.md) |\n| DSH plugin development guide (the local reference layer for the community guides) | [docs/plugin-dev/README.md (available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/plugin-dev/README.md) |\n| Standards and checker author reference (ENTRY / FLOW / BOOST, DS-C, checker) | [docs/plugin-dev/standards/README.md (available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/plugin-dev/standards/README.md) |\n| Extension protocol v1 overview | [docs/extension-protocol/README.md (available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/extension-protocol/README.md) |\n| Enterprise publishing, review and permissions | [docs/enterprise-publishing.md (available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/enterprise-publishing.md) |\n\nUpstream tools are pinned: `dsh-primer` ([npm package page](https://www.npmjs.com/package/dsh-primer)) and `dsh-standards@1.6.9` ([npm v1.6.9](https://www.npmjs.com/package/dsh-standards/v/1.6.9)); dsh-ee only pins versions and shows usage, it does not reimplement upstream check logic.\n",
  "harness-quickstart": "# Connect a third-party Harness or your own client\n\n## The five-minute path (H1: just `/mcp`)\n\nFor a third-party Harness (Claude Code, Codex, your own Agent, scripts) connecting to dsh-ee, the shortest path is **H1: one `ee_at_` token + `/mcp`**. Follow the repository docs for H2 when you need skill files, a local extension on the machine, or a single command that generates the config; the DSH-only deep integration (H3) is not offered for third-party clients, so do not design an integration around H3.\n\n| Step | What to do | Where to read |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Create an `ee_at_` token in \"My tokens\"; keep scopes minimal for the purpose | \"Tokens and scopes\" below |\n| 2 | Read the anonymous discovery document `GET /.well-known/dsh-ee` and confirm `api_version`, the device code endpoint, capabilities and limits | \"Discovery and version\" |\n| 3 | Call `/v1/models` and `/v1/chat/completions` with the `models` scope | \"Model gateway\" |\n| 4 | Connect to `/mcp` with the `connections` scope and call `tools/list` dynamically per session | \"MCP\" |\n| 5 | Write tools go through server-side confirmation: first get the \"confirm on the web\" link, then call again with identical parameters after the employee approves | \"Write operations\" |\n\n## Tokens and scopes\n\nTokens start with `ee_at_` and map to an individual user; scopes are only an upper bound — every request is additionally intersected with the account's permissions on the server (if the account does not have the permission code, the token still cannot use it).\n\n| Purpose | Required scope | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Call models | `models` | `/v1/models`, `/v1/chat/completions`; the account also needs the \"Call models\" permission |\n| Use company connection, knowledge base and app data tools through `/mcp` | `connections` | The `/mcp` entry checks `connections` |\n| Use the `ee_*` Workbench tools in `/mcp` | `connections` + `agent` | The global Agent switch in system settings must also be on |\n| Read the capability directory (optional) | `hub` | Apps and revisions in `/v1/apps` and `/v1/capabilities` |\n\nTreat tokens like passwords: read them from environment variables or a credential store, never put them in repositories, logs or screenshots; issue one per integrator and revoke per person.\n\n## Discovery and version\n\nThe anonymous endpoint `GET /.well-known/dsh-ee` returns `api_version`, the device code endpoint, `scopes_supported`, model/MCP/app capabilities, request size limits and the deprecation policy. `GET /v1/capabilities` (Bearer optional) returns the models, tool counts and revisions visible to the current token; both responses carry the `Dsh-EE-API-Version: 1` header.\n\nDevice code sign-in is a public optional entry: `POST /api/device/code` returns `device_code` / `user_code`; after the employee confirms on the `/device` page, poll `POST /api/device/token` at the returned `interval`. A device code is valid for 10 minutes and the token can only be fetched once; there is no refresh token, so restart the flow after expiry.\n\n## Model gateway (`/v1`)\n\nOpenAI-compatible; minimal call:\n\n```sh\nexport BASE='https://<dsh-ee>'\nexport TOKEN='ee_at_…'\n\ncurl -fsS \"$BASE/v1/models\" -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\"\n\ncurl -fsS \"$BASE/v1/chat/completions\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" \\\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{\"model\":\"<模型名>\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"你好\"}]}'\n```\n\nModel names follow what `/v1/models` returns; streaming, rate limits and error codes follow the OpenAI-compatible conventions.\n\n## MCP (`/mcp`)\n\n`/mcp` is Streamable HTTP + JSON-RPC 2.0: `POST /mcp` sends requests with `Accept: application/json, text/event-stream`; the `Mcp-Session-Id` returned by `initialize` is used for subsequent requests.\n\n```sh\ncurl -fsS \"$BASE/mcp\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" \\\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n  -H 'Accept: application/json, text/event-stream' \\\n  -d '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"initialize\",\"params\":{\"protocolVersion\":\"2025-06-18\",\"capabilities\":{},\"clientInfo\":{\"name\":\"my-harness\",\"version\":\"1.0\"}}}'\n```\n\n| Tool prefix | Source |\n| --- | --- |\n| `<connection-name>__<tool-name>` | Tools in company connections |\n| `kb__*` | Knowledge base tools |\n| `app__*` / `data__*` | Tool surfaces of micro-apps |\n| `ee_*` | Workbench tools (need the `agent` scope and the global Agent switch) |\n\nVisible tools depend on the permissions of the token's owner, so different employees may see different results: **call `tools/list` dynamically in each session, never hard-code it**. Treat a tool as a write tool when `annotations.readOnlyHint` is missing.\n\n## Write operations: server-side confirmation\n\nBy default (`non_dsh`), a third-party Harness cannot execute write tools directly: `tools/call` returns `isError: true` and a \"confirm on the web\" link. After the employee checks the parameters and approves on the `/confirm/<id>` page, call again with **exactly the same parameters** to actually execute; one approval works once, is valid for 10 minutes, and any changed parameter requires a new confirmation. DSH clients may bypass this according to their own confirmation setting, but do not assume a third-party Harness has that shortcut.\n\n## Limits\n\n| Limit | Notes |\n| --- | --- |\n| Not covered by H1 | Skill directories, the Workbench, micro-app web pages, and automatic plugin install and update are DSH-only deep integration; third-party Harnesses must not assume them |\n| Request size | `max_request_bytes` / `max_response_bytes` follow the discovery document |\n| Token lifecycle | No refresh token; reissue after expiry; the device code flow can only fetch a token once |\n| Scopes and permissions | Scopes are an upper bound; the effective permission is \"account permissions ∩ scopes\" |\n| API stability | The existing shapes of `/v1`, `/.well-known/dsh-ee` and `/mcp` stay unchanged; new capabilities follow the discovery document |\n| Write operations | Anything without `readOnlyHint` is treated as a write operation and goes through the confirmation flow |\n\n## Full text in the repository\n\nThis page is only a guide; protocol and endpoint details live in the repository docs (docs marked \"available after merge\" are introduced by the companion docs PR):\n\n- [Third-party Harness access (docs/harness-integration.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/harness-integration.md)\n- [Non-DSH client access L1 (docs/l1-client.md)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/l1-client.md)\n- [Discovery and capabilities (docs/l1-client.md §2, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/l1-client.md#2-%E5%8F%91%E7%8E%B0%E4%B8%8E%E8%83%BD%E5%8A%9B%E4%B8%AD%E7%AB%8B%E5%A5%91%E7%BA%A6-v1)\n- [The Harness page in the developer docs (docs/dev/harness.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/dev/harness.md)\n- [Extension protocol overview (docs/extension-protocol/README.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/extension-protocol/README.md)\n",
  "anthropic-api": "# Anthropic Messages compatibility (POST /v1/messages)\n\nBeyond the OpenAI-compatible gateway, dsh-ee also offers a model-calling entry in Anthropic Messages format: Claude Code, the Anthropic SDK,\nor any client written against the Anthropic Messages protocol can use **dsh-ee's unified access token** to call the models the company has connected directly.\nRequests are translated into a single OpenAI chat completions call and handed to the existing gateway, so model permissions, account/token monthly quota, upstream failover,\nusage accounting and auditing are exactly the same as for the OpenAI gateway.\n\n**v0 covers**: non-streaming and streaming (SSE) `POST /v1/messages`, auth, request/response envelopes and error envelopes.\n**Not covered**: `count_tokens`, Batches, Files, full beta headers, and execution of server-side tools (`web_search` etc.).\n\n## 1. Connect with Claude Code\n\nTreat dsh-ee as the Anthropic API base URL, and use dsh-ee's **public model names** as model names (the `id` returned by `GET /v1/models`,\nnot the upstream vendor's name):\n\n```sh\nexport ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL='https://dsh-ee.example.com'\nexport ANTHROPIC_API_KEY='ee_at_…'            # 客户端发 x-api-key\n# 或者：export ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN='ee_at_…'   # 客户端发 Authorization: Bearer\n\n# 关键：Claude Code 默认的 claude-* 模型名在 dsh-ee 里不存在，必须显式指定对外模型名\nexport ANTHROPIC_MODEL='company-chat'\nexport ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL='company-chat'\nexport ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_SONNET_MODEL='company-chat'\nexport ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_OPUS_MODEL='company-chat'\n```\n\n> dsh-ee **does not** map `claude-*` names: the model name must be one of the models available to the account/token, otherwise you get a 403.\n> Anthropic-compatible layers from vendors such as DeepSeek automatically map claude names to their own models; dsh-ee's models are multi-vendor models\n> connected by administrators and have no single mapping target, so it explicitly requires public model names.\n\nThere are two equivalent spellings of the base URL (same handler; behavior, auth and error envelopes are exactly the same):\n\n| `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` | What the client actually requests | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `https://dsh-ee.example.com` | `POST /v1/messages` | Standard Anthropic base URL form |\n| `https://dsh-ee.example.com/anthropic` | `POST /anthropic/v1/messages` | Matches DeepSeek's Anthropic base URL convention (`<base>/anthropic`) |\n\nYou can also verify it directly with curl:\n\n```sh\ncurl -fsS \"$BASE/v1/messages\" \\\n  -H \"x-api-key: $TOKEN\" \\\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{\n    \"model\": \"company-chat\",\n    \"max_tokens\": 256,\n    \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"ping\"}]}]\n  }'\n\n# 同一个端点的别名（DeepSeek 风格 base URL：ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=$BASE/anthropic）\ncurl -fsS \"$BASE/anthropic/v1/messages\" ...  # 参数与上面完全相同\n```\n\n## 2. Authentication and permissions\n\n- Credentials: `x-api-key: <token>` or `Authorization: Bearer <token>`; the two are equivalent, and `x-api-key` wins when both are sent.\n- Tokens: the unified access token `ee_at_…` (the legacy `sk-ee-…` / `ee_npm_…` are also accepted, with the same behavior as the OpenAI gateway).\n- Permissions: the token needs the `models` scope, the account needs the \"Call models\" (`llm.use`) permission, and the model must be within the account's and the token's available range.\n- Quota: the account/token monthly quota and quota expiry are shared with the OpenAI gateway; rejected requests are not forwarded upstream and are not accounted.\n- Rate limiting: shares the fixed-window per-token rate limit with `/v1/*` (60 requests per minute by default, adjusted with `DSH_EE_RATE_LIMIT`).\n  Requests carrying only `x-api-key` are still counted against the token's quota and do not fall back to counting by IP; over-limit requests also return the Anthropic error envelope and `Retry-After`.\n- `anthropic-version` / `anthropic-beta`: the DeepSeek native passthrough path passes these two headers **unchanged** to the upstream (whether they are supported is up to DeepSeek; dsh-ee does not parse them); the fallback conversion path for other providers ignores them without reporting an error.\n- **The Files API is not in v0 scope**: sending `anthropic-beta: files-api-2025-04-14` still does not make dsh-ee implement Files; file references with `image.source.type=file` on the fallback conversion path return 400 (images only support `base64` / `url`), while on the native passthrough path file/vision blocks are handed to DeepSeek unchanged; the Files / Batches endpoints return 404.\n- Service discovery: `models.anthropic_messages_endpoint` in `GET /.well-known/dsh-ee` gives this endpoint's address,\n  and `GET /v1/openapi.json` also contains the request/response/error descriptions for `POST /v1/messages`.\n\n## 3. Upstream forwarding: DeepSeek native passthrough / conversion fallback for other providers\n\ndsh-ee decides how a request reaches the upstream based on the candidate routes of the public model (the candidate order is the priority in `/admin/providers`):\n\n| `provider.vendor` of the first candidate route | Forwarding mode | What dsh-ee does |\n|---|---|---|\n| `deepseek` | **Native passthrough**: `POST <provider.base>/anthropic/v1/messages` | Only auth, model permission, quota, rate limiting and `model` rewriting (public name → upstream name); passes `anthropic-version` / `anthropic-beta` through unchanged; forwards the remaining request body fields (server tools, citations, safety, title, latest_reminder, file/vision blocks, etc.) unchanged; forwards successful JSON/SSE responses **byte for byte**; parses usage out-of-band for accounting (estimates when usage is absent); forwards upstream errors with the original status/body. |\n| Others | **Conversion fallback**: Anthropic Messages → OpenAI chat completions | See §4 \"Request conversion\" and §5 \"Response and streaming\"; `anthropic-version` / `anthropic-beta` are ignored; unsupported blocks return 400 or are ignored. |\n\n- Under native passthrough, dsh-ee **does not validate** generation parameters such as `max_tokens` / `temperature` (that is left to DeepSeek) and does not parse content blocks; monthly quota, per-token rate limiting and model permissions are still enforced by dsh-ee.\n- **Failover only happens within the same protocol**: if the first route is `deepseek`, it only switches among other `deepseek` routes; otherwise it only switches among conversion routes of other vendors, and **never across protocols**; if all fail, the last route's error is returned. When one public model has several routes, the first one (highest priority) decides the protocol.\n- Native passthrough also supports the `/anthropic/v1/messages` alias; `x-api-key` / Bearer authentication is exactly the same as in the conversion layer.\n- The discovery document's `models.anthropic_messages_native_vendors` (default `[\"deepseek\"]`) and `models.anthropic_messages_conversion_fallback` (`openai-chat`) announce this policy.\n\n## 4. Request conversion (fallback conversion path: Anthropic → OpenAI chat)\n\n| Anthropic field | Handling |\n|---|---|\n| `model` | Checks permission against the dsh-ee public model name and replaces it with the upstream model name when forwarding |\n| `max_tokens` | Required positive integer; the per-request limit matches the OpenAI gateway (32768); over the limit returns 400 |\n| `system` | A string or an array of text blocks is merged into a single OpenAI `system` message |\n| `messages[].content` | A string passes through unchanged; content blocks are converted per the table below |\n| `temperature` / `top_p` | Passed through unchanged (`temperature` range is decided by the upstream) |\n| `top_k` | OpenAI chat has no corresponding parameter; ignored |\n| `stop_sequences` | The array is converted to OpenAI `stop` |\n| `tools` | Client tools `{name, description, input_schema}` → `{type:\"function\", function:{name, description, parameters}}` |\n| `tool_choice` | `auto`→`auto`, `any`→`required`, `none`→`none`, `tool`→the specified function; `disable_parallel_tool_use`→`parallel_tool_calls=false` |\n| `metadata.user_id` | Converted to OpenAI's `user` so the upstream can rate-limit/isolate by end user |\n| `thinking` | `enabled` / `adaptive` → OpenAI extension parameter `thinking:{\"type\":\"enabled\"}`; `disabled` → `thinking:{\"type\":\"disabled\"}`; `budget_tokens` ignored |\n| `reasoning_effort` / `output_config.effort` | Converted to OpenAI `reasoning_effort`; `none` turns thinking off; `xhigh` / `max` / `ultra` normalize to `high` (range `minimal/low/medium/high`) |\n| `stream` | Forwards as streaming; automatically adds `stream_options.include_usage=true` for accounting |\n| `container` / `mcp_servers` / `service_tier` / `top_k` / `cache_control` | Ignored |\n\nContent blocks:\n\n| Block type | Position | Conversion |\n|---|---|---|\n| `text` | user / assistant | Text (multiple text blocks in one message are joined with newlines) |\n| `image` | user | `source.type=base64` / `url` → OpenAI `image_url` content part (the upstream must support vision) |\n| `tool_use` | assistant | → OpenAI `tool_calls` (`input` serialized into an `arguments` JSON string) |\n| `tool_result` | user | → OpenAI `role:\"tool\"` message (`tool_use_id` → `tool_call_id`; content flattened into text, images replaced by `[image]` placeholders) |\n| `thinking` | assistant | → the assistant message's `reasoning_content` (upstreams such as DeepSeek require it back in follow-up turns with tools) |\n| `redacted_thinking` | assistant | Cannot be restored to text; ignored |\n| `document` and other blocks | — | 400 `invalid_request_error` (v0 does not support file/document blocks) |\n\nServer-side tools (tools executed by Anthropic, whose `type` is `web_search_20250305`, `bash_20250124`, etc.) are **ignored**\nrather than failing the whole request — this keeps other Claude Code features usable, but web search/code execution tools do not actually take effect.\n\n## 5. Response and streaming (fallback conversion path)\n\nA non-streaming response is a standard Anthropic `message`: `id` (`msg_…`), `type:\"message\"`, `role:\"assistant\"`,\n`model` (the public model name requested by the client), the `content` block array, `stop_reason`, `stop_sequence` and `usage`.\n\n- `content` order: `thinking` (when the upstream returns `reasoning_content` / `reasoning`) → `text` → `tool_use`.\n  The `signature` of a thinking block is an opaque placeholder generated by dsh-ee (the server does not verify signatures); the client can send it back unchanged.\n- `stop_reason`: tool calls → `tool_use`; `finish_reason=length` → `max_tokens`; `content_filter` → `refusal`;\n  anything else → `end_turn`. OpenAI cannot distinguish a `stop_sequences` hit, so `stop_sequence` is never returned.\n- `usage`: `output_tokens` takes the upstream `completion_tokens`; `input_tokens = prompt_tokens - cache hits`,\n  `cache_read_input_tokens` takes the upstream `prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens` (0 when the field is absent),\n  and `cache_creation_input_tokens` is fixed at 0. **Accounting** still counts the upstream `prompt_tokens + completion_tokens` against the monthly quota.\n\nStreaming uses `Content-Type: text/event-stream`, with this event sequence (the same as Anthropic's official one):\n\n```text\nevent: message_start          # message 骨架，usage.input_tokens 是估算值\nevent: content_block_start    # 依次出现 thinking / text / tool_use 块\nevent: content_block_delta    # thinking_delta / text_delta / input_json_delta / signature_delta\nevent: content_block_stop\nevent: message_delta          # delta.stop_reason + usage（最终用量，以这里为准）\nevent: message_stop\n```\n\n- OpenAI's `data: [DONE]` is not sent; a connection that drops before `message_stop` means the response is incomplete.\n- `message_start.usage.input_tokens` is an estimate from the request body (the upstream usage is not known when the stream starts);\n  the `input_tokens` / `output_tokens` in `message_delta.usage` are the final values.\n- When the upstream gives no streaming usage (for example, the client stops midway), dsh-ee estimates accounting from the request and the content already forwarded, so quota is not undercounted.\n\n## 6. Errors\n\nThe error body has the Anthropic shape, with HTTP status codes corresponding to `error.type`:\n\n```json\n{\"type\":\"error\",\"error\":{\"type\":\"rate_limit_error\",\"message\":\"请求过于频繁，请稍后重试\"}}\n```\n\n| HTTP | `error.type` | Trigger |\n|---|---|---|\n| 400 | `invalid_request_error` | Request body is not JSON, missing `model` / `max_tokens`, parameter out of range, unsupported block type |\n| 401 | `authentication_error` | Missing or invalid token |\n| 403 | `permission_error` | Token lacks the `models` scope, account lacks `llm.use`, model not in range, quota expired |\n| 404 | `not_found_error` | The public model has no available upstream; compatible endpoints that are not implemented, such as `count_tokens` |\n| 413 | `request_too_large` | Request body exceeds 16 MiB |\n| 429 | `rate_limit_error` | Account/token monthly quota exhausted, per-token request rate limit, upstream returned 429 |\n| 5xx | `api_error` / `overloaded_error` | All upstream routes unavailable, upstream 5xx, upstream response cannot be parsed |\n\n`/v1/messages/count_tokens` authenticates first and then returns 404 `not_found_error` (not implemented in v0),\nrather than falling through to the OpenAI error envelope at `/v1/`.\n\nOn the DeepSeek native passthrough path, errors returned by the upstream (DeepSeek itself uses the Anthropic error envelope) are forwarded with the **original status code and body**\nand are not re-wrapped; errors produced by dsh-ee itself (auth/permission/quota/rate limit/no upstream for the model/upstream unreachable) still use the table above.\n\n## 7. Known limits (v0)\n\n- Only `POST /v1/messages` (and the alias `POST /anthropic/v1/messages`) is implemented; `count_tokens` (including its alias),\n  Batches, Files, `/v1/messages/batches` and the like return 404.\n- Server-side tools are not executed (see §3); `container`, `mcp_servers`, `service_tier` and `top_k` are ignored.\n- No `claude-*` model name mapping: the client must configure the model name as a public model name from `/v1/models`.\n- Thinking content is recognized through the upstream `reasoning_content` / `reasoning` fields; when the upstream returns no thinking content,\n  the response has no `thinking` block. `budget_tokens` has no effect; control thinking depth with `reasoning_effort` / `output_config.effort`.\n- Images are converted to OpenAI's `image_url` content part, and whether the upstream model supports images is up to the upstream; file blocks such as `document` are not supported.\n- A `stop_sequences` hit and a normal finish are both `finish_reason=stop` upstream, so they cannot be distinguished and both return `end_turn`.\n- No signature verification: the `signature` of a thinking block is a placeholder value, and the server neither verifies nor relies on it.\n- The DeepSeek native passthrough path does not validate generation parameters or parse content blocks in dsh-ee; limits such as `max_tokens` are decided by DeepSeek;\n  upstream errors are forwarded unchanged; responses on the native path do not go through dsh-ee's Anthropic conversion layer.\n- Failover does not cross protocols: once the higher-priority route decides the protocol, a lower-priority route using the other protocol is never tried.\n\n## 8. Related\n\n- OpenAI-compatible gateway, tokens and scopes: [Harness access](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/dev/harness.md)\n- Full third-party client manual and error table: [l1-client.md](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/l1-client.md)\n- Model gateway error code index: [errors.md](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/errors.md)\n- Implementation: `server/internal/api/anthropic.go`, `server/internal/api/anthropic_convert.go`\n",
  "plugin-quickstart": "# Write a DSH plugin and publish it to the company registry\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n| Dependency | Version | Why it is needed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Node | ≥ 24.9.0 | Both `dsh-primer` and `dsh-standards` require ≥24.9 in `engines`; the DSH 0.2.2 line also requires Node ≥24.9, while earlier 0.2.x lines can run on 22.19+ |\n| DSH | 0.2.x line (0.2.1–0.2.2) | Run plugins and `dsh plugin add`; `dsh-primer` declares support for `>=0.2.1-0 <0.3.0`, so fill in `engines.dsh` with the range you actually verified |\n| pnpm | Any recent version | Install dependencies and package |\n| dsh-primer | 0.4.x | Scaffold, static checks and install smoke test (formerly `dsh-plugin-guide` 0.3.x, old CLI alias `dsh-plugin-dev`) |\n| Company account | Has the \"Publish capabilities\" permission (`registry.publish`) | Upload plugins and request community plugins |\n\n## Scaffold in five minutes\n\n```bash\n# 0. dsh-primer 是可安装的 DSH bundle，不是 DSH 自带命令；先装进你用的 profile\ndsh plugin --profile web add dsh-primer\n\n# 1. 生成骨架（上游模板，TypeScript）\ndsh-primer new my-plugin\n\n# 2. 本地装依赖并跑通\ncd my-plugin\npnpm install\npnpm build\n\n# 3. 自查包形状（静态检查，在本地执行）\ndsh-primer check\n\n# 4. 装进一个测试 profile 验证加载/卸载\ndsh-primer verify\n```\n\n`dsh-primer` belongs to the upstream toolchain; dsh-ee only pins its version and shows usage, and does not reimplement its check logic.\n\n## Manifest fields the company cares about most\n\n| Field | What to write | Why the company side looks at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `name` | Package name under the company scope, e.g. `@company/hello` | Decides whether it can be routed to the company npm registry |\n| `version` | Semantic version, one version per publish | A version number never rolls back once taken; publish conflicts reject the submission |\n| `main` / `files` | The real entry point and file allowlist to publish | Avoid shipping source, tests and `.tgz` files together |\n| `cordis.patch.yml` | The plugin's patch declarations for Cordis | The precheck inspects the bundle and patch structure |\n| `dsh.bundle` | DSH plugin manifest (if any) | Decides whether the client recognizes this package |\n| `engines.dsh` | The actually verified DSH version range | Checked against the company's DSH version line; a missing declaration gets a warning |\n| `peerDependencies` | DSH-related peers | The precheck checks whether the peer range resolves |\n| `license` | An SPDX identifier such as `Apache-2.0` | A missing license is a **warning** in the precheck (not blocking), but it still goes to manual review |\n\n## What the local self-checks cover\n\n| Command | What it checks | Where to run it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `dsh-primer check` | Whether the package shape and entry points are complete | Author machine or author repo CI |\n| `dsh-primer verify` | Smoke test of install, enable and uninstall | Author machine or author repo CI |\n| `npx dsh-standards@1.6.9 check .` | Static evidence for upstream standard clauses (requires Node ≥24.9) | Author machine or author repo CI; the result is author-provided evidence |\n\nThe dsh-ee server does not execute plugin code, nor does it run these checkers; the \"Share a plugin\" precheck on the web only looks at the static shape of the package itself and the company admission conditions.\n\n## Publish to the company registry\n\n| Entry | How | Who it is for |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| \"Share a plugin\" in the Workbench | Upload the `.tgz`, review the precheck results, then submit | First publish, when you want to see the precheck first |\n| npm direct publish | `npm publish --registry <company registry>`; create the token in \"My tokens\" | Existing CI pipelines |\n| Agent API | `POST /api/agent/packages`, called with a company token | Automated publishing |\n\nVersion numbers are taken during review and publishing; duplicate versions or conflicts are rejected. The precheck returns three conclusion fields: `can_submit` (no publish conflicts; the publish API will accept it), `will_publish` (no fail items under the open admission mode; published immediately), `requires_review` (goes to review after submission).\n\n## FAQ\n\n| Symptom | Common cause | What to do |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Precheck reports missing entry files | The `files` allowlist does not include build output, or `main` points to an unbuilt file | Run `pnpm build` first, then check `files` |\n| Precheck has fail items | bundle / patch structure errors, suspected secrets, etc. | Fix before submitting; you can submit with fail items, but the version number is taken and review usually will not pass |\n| Precheck has only warnings | Missing license, no `engines.dsh`, install scripts, etc. | Not blocking: allowlist mode goes to manual review, open mode can publish directly |\n| Peer range does not resolve | Two incompatible DSH version lines are declared at the same time | Keep only one version line, or complete a range that resolves |\n| Node version error on install | `engines.node` does not match the employee's Node | Fix `engines.node` according to what you actually verified (example: `^22.19.0 \\|\\| >=24.9.0`) |\n| Cannot install inside the company | Token permissions are insufficient or npmrc is not configured for the company registry | Check the \"Install capabilities\" permission and `.npmrc` |\n\n## Full text in the repository\n\nThis page is only a guide; the full text and licenses live in the repository docs (links point to the docs after merge):\n\n- [Quickstart (docs/dev/quickstart.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/dev/quickstart.md)\n- [DSH plugin publishing (docs/dev/plugin-publish.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/dev/plugin-publish.md)\n- [Errors and FAQ (docs/dev/errors-faq.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/dev/errors-faq.md)\n- [Plugin author guide (docs/plugin-authors.md)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/plugin-authors.md)\n- [DSH plugin development guide (docs/plugin-dev/README.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/plugin-dev/README.md)\n- [Upstream: dsh-primer repository](https://github.com/PerryLink/dsh-primer) · [npm package page](https://www.npmjs.com/package/dsh-primer)\n- [Upstream: dsh-plugin-guide (pointer page for the old 0.3.x name)](https://github.com/PerryLink/dsh-plugin-guide)\n",
  "extension-quickstart": "# Write a dsh-ee extension: manifest, checks and calls\n\n## Four forms\n\ndsh-ee extensions bring company systems in. Choose the form first: the first three are delivered as a manifest, and the fourth is an official integration implemented by dsh-ee itself, for which authors do not write a manifest.\n\n| Form | Manifest `kind` | Where it runs | Deliverable | How dsh-ee calls it |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Declarative | `declarative` | HTTP requests sent from inside the dsh-ee process | Manifest + OpenAPI document (zip) | Generates tools from the OpenAPI document and calls the source system directly |\n| Service | `service` | An HTTP service the company deploys itself | A service that implements the manifest and the agreed interface | Fetches `/.well-known/dsh-ee-extension.json`; tools go through `POST /mcp` |\n| Local | `local` | An npm package on employee machines | npm package + manifest | The Agent connects to the local MCP directly; write operations ask dsh-ee for confirmation first, and a summary is reported after the call |\n| Built-in | (no manifest) | Go inside the dsh-ee process | Ships with dsh-ee upgrades | Same internal interface as service extensions, no network (e.g. a Feishu document knowledge source) |\n\nThe manifest schema only accepts the three `kind` values `declarative` / `service` / `local`; \"built-in\" is implemented by dsh-ee itself and cannot be delivered through a manifest. The server does not run third-party code: for declarative extensions dsh-ee sends the requests, service extensions run in the company's own service, and local extensions run on employee machines.\n\n## Scaffold an extension\n\n```bash\n# 声明式：不写代码，有 OpenAPI 就能接\ndsh-ee extension init ./my-ext --id acme/my-ext --kind declarative\n\n# 服务式：要写逻辑，企业自己部署\ndsh-ee extension init ./my-service --id acme/my-service --kind service\n```\n\n`init` must specify the identity with `--id <author>/<name>`; `--kind` accepts `declarative` (default) or `service`. Copy local extensions from the [`extensions/official/legacy-local`](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/extensions/official/legacy-local) template or example in the repository. The generated directory contains an `AGENTS.md`; open it with an Agent and it knows the next step.\n\n| Command | Purpose | What it does locally |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `dsh-ee extension init` | Copies an extension from the example | Writes files |\n| `dsh-ee schema list` / `schema get manifest` | Look up specifications such as the manifest and error codes offline | Read-only |\n| `dsh-ee extension check` | Statically checks the manifest, API document, read-only flags and credential usage | Read-only checks, no code execution |\n| `dsh-ee extension test` | Starts the service extension and tests it item by item against the protocol | Starts a local process |\n| `dsh-ee extension dev` | Starts a mock dsh-ee (no database needed) | Starts a local process |\n| `dsh-ee extension call` | Calls an extension tool once as a given employee | Read-only diagnostics |\n| `dsh-ee extension pack` | Packs a **declarative** extension into a zip and computes its checksum | Writes a zip |\n| `dsh-ee extension report` | Generates a delivery note for administrators | Writes files |\n\n`check` exits with code 1 when there are errors, and every result carries `code`, `severity`, `where`, `message` and `fix`. All the commands above run on the author's machine or in the author's repo CI; the dsh-ee server does not execute third-party code, and does not run checkers in the web page either.\n\n## Minimal manifest example\n\nThe manifest below matches the shape generated by `dsh-ee extension init --kind declarative`; after pairing it with an `openapi.yaml` covered by `servers`, `dsh-ee extension check` reports 0 errors and 0 warnings. Fields follow `dsh-ee schema get manifest`; the manifest is a strict schema, and an extra field not in the specification is an error too.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"acme/oa\",\n  \"version\": \"0.1.0\",\n  \"name\": \"Acme OA\",\n  \"description\": \"查询和提交 Acme OA 的审批单。\",\n  \"kind\": \"declarative\",\n  \"openapi\": \"openapi.yaml\",\n  \"servers\": [\"https://oa.acme.internal\"],\n  \"credential\": { \"mode\": \"ee_jwt\" },\n  \"tools\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"list_items\",\n      \"readOnly\": true,\n      \"description\": \"列出当前员工的条目\",\n      \"keep\": [\"items.id\", \"items.title\", \"total\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"create_item\",\n      \"description\": \"新建一个条目。这是写操作，调用前要员工确认。\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nA few easy mistakes:\n\n- `id` must be written as \"author/name\" using only lowercase letters, digits and hyphens; `version` is a strict semantic version.\n- A declarative extension must have `openapi` (a relative path inside the extension directory) and at least one `servers` entry; `servers[0].url` in the OpenAPI document must fall within the manifest's `servers`.\n- `tools[].name` corresponds to each OpenAPI operation's `operationId`; mark query tools as `readOnly`, and do not mark write operations.\n- The credential mode (`credential.mode`) is only needed at call time: `shared` / `user_secret` / `ee_jwt` / `oauth`; choose the one the other side accepts.\n\n## Confirm before delivery\n\n| Check item | Where to confirm |\n| --- | --- |\n| Manifest fields and protocol version | `dsh-ee schema get manifest`, `dsh-ee extension check --json` |\n| API document and tool mapping | The `tools` results from `check`; whether write operations are marked `readOnly` |\n| Credentials only come from dsh-ee, never written into the manifest | No plaintext key appears in the manifest or the code |\n| Protocol consistency of the service interface | Test cases from `dsh-ee extension test` |\n| Declarative packaging | `dsh-ee extension pack` produces a zip; service extensions deliver a code repository or image |\n| Who can use which tools | Administrators configure it in \"Connections\" and \"Fields & access rules\" |\n\n## Relationship to DSH skills\n\nTo give the Agent an executable instruction, dsh-ee wrote the extension development flow as a skill (`dsh-ee-extension-dev`) and distributes it to DSH under the \"Develop extensions\" (`extensions.develop`) permission. People and Agents see the same steps: the page is for humans to read, and the skill is for the Agent to execute.\n\n## Full text in the repository\n\nThis page is only a guide; protocol details, install review and error codes follow the repository docs (links point to the docs after merge):\n\n- [Extension development (docs/dev/extensions.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/dev/extensions.md)\n- [Extension protocol v1 overview (docs/extension-protocol/README.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/extension-protocol/README.md)\n- [Extension protocol text (docs/extension-protocol.md)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/extension-protocol.md)\n- [Enterprise publishing, review and permissions (docs/enterprise-publishing.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/enterprise-publishing.md)\n- [Extension directory and example index (extensions/README.md)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/extensions/README.md)\n- [Manifest JSON Schema (extensions/schemas/manifest.schema.json)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/extensions/schemas/manifest.schema.json)\n- [Design trade-offs (docs/plugins.md; a design draft, not user documentation)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/plugins.md)\n",
  "access-rules": "# Permissions and access rules: a quick reference\n\n## The three layers\n\nAccess control in dsh-ee has three layers. All of them are changed in the admin console and take effect immediately after saving.\n\n| Layer | Question it answers | Where to configure | Who is affected after configuring |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Permission code | Can this person use this feature | \"Roles & permissions\" | Page visibility, API calls |\n| Fields & access rules | Can this field and this record be viewed or changed | \"Fields & access rules\" | Custom fields on documents, knowledge bases, users, etc. |\n| Admin constraints | Does changing critical configuration need approval | \"Fields & access rules\" | Operations that change field values or configuration |\n\n## How permission codes reach pages and APIs\n\nMenus only decide what is visible; the real boundary is in the API. Hiding a button on a page is not a security boundary.\n\n| Common permission code | What it does | Default for employees |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `portal.view` | Use employee pages such as the Workbench, company apps and developer docs | Yes |\n| `registry.publish` | Publish plugins, skills, prompts and apps | Yes |\n| `extensions.develop` | Get the extension development skill and command reference | No; an administrator must grant it |\n| `plugins.manage` | Review, publish and unpublish capabilities | No |\n| `connections.manage` | Connect and configure company connections | No |\n\nPermission codes are synced into the database from the built-in directory in code; permission codes added by a new release are granted to the \"Employee\" role automatically (if the directory marks a default role).\n\n## Order in which access rules are evaluated\n\n1. Super admin: not limited by rules (unless super admin is turned off in the system).\n2. Role permissions: first decide whether the feature itself is visible.\n3. Field access rules: decide read / write / deny field by field; rules can carry conditions.\n4. Admin constraints: whether a write operation must first submit a change, and who approves it.\n\nOnce an earlier layer reaches a conclusion, later layers do not loosen it.\n\n## Configuration entry points and examples\n\n| I want to | Go to |\n| --- | --- |\n| Give a group of employees access to extension development materials | \"Roles & permissions\" → grant `extensions.develop` |\n| Let only this department see a field | \"Fields & access rules\" → create a read rule and choose department as the condition |\n| Forbid exporting the \"Confidential\" field in documents | Same as above: add a deny rule and check the API response |\n| Require approval before changing a critical field | \"Fields & access rules\" → Admin constraints → bind a change request |\n\n## How to fit in when writing extensions\n\n- Declare the permission codes and credential names you need in the manifest; do not put credentials in code or the manifest.\n- Field read/write rules are configured by administrators; extensions only handle the visible fields returned by the API and do not guess permissions themselves.\n- Error codes distinguish \"no permission\", \"not found\" and \"bad parameter\"; do not return all of them as 500.\n\n## Full text in the repository\n\nThis page is only a quick reference; rule details, extension install approval and the permission code directory live in the repository docs (links point to the docs after merge):\n\n- [Access control design (docs/access-control.md)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/access-control.md)\n- [Enterprise publishing, review and permissions (docs/enterprise-publishing.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/enterprise-publishing.md)\n- [Extension protocol v1 overview · credentials and write operations (docs/extension-protocol/README.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/extension-protocol/README.md)\n- [Extension protocol text (docs/extension-protocol.md)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/extension-protocol.md)\n- [Access configuration skill (extensions/skills/dsh-ee-access-config/SKILL.md)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/extensions/skills/dsh-ee-access-config/SKILL.md)\n",
  "publish-precheck": "# Publish entries and how to read the precheck\n\n## Three publish entries\n\n| Entry | Path | Good for | When the precheck runs |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Web upload | \"Share a plugin\" in the Workbench | First publish, when you want to fix things while watching the precheck | Runs right after upload |\n| npm direct publish | `npm publish --registry <company registry>` | Existing CI, publishing along a version pipeline | Runs at publish time |\n| Agent API | `POST /api/agent/packages` | Automated publishing and bulk migration | Runs at publish time |\n\nAll three entries follow the same admission rules: the package first goes through a static precheck, then publish conflicts are checked, and finally the admission mode decides whether it goes live directly or enters review.\n\n## What the precheck scans\n\nThe precheck only unpacks the package for static checks; it **does not execute plugin code**, does not write to the database, and does not reserve a version number. Results come in three levels: pass / warn / fail.\n\n| Scan key | What it cares about | Severity |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `bundle` | `dsh.bundle` and patch files: missing, unsafe paths, files not in the package | fail for plugin packages; a warning when missing for non-plugin packages |\n| `patch` | Structure of `cordis.patch.yml` (array, `insert`, `id`/`name`, duplicate ids) | structural errors are fail; name mismatches, etc. are warnings |\n| `cordis` | Whether Cordis-related packages are mixed into `dependencies` | warning |\n| `cordis-peer` | Whether the Cordis range in `peerDependencies` deviates from the recommended value | warning |\n| `skills` | Skill manifest distributed with the package | warning (a hint that review will see it) |\n| `install-scripts` | Install-time scripts (`preinstall`, etc.) | warning |\n| `secrets` | Whether the package contains suspected secrets | **fail** |\n| `license` | Whether an SPDX license is declared | **warning** (not blocking) |\n| `engines` | Whether the plugin declares `engines.dsh` | warning |\n| `dependencies` | List of dependencies outside the company scope | info |\n| `size` | Package size (warning above 20 MB) | warning |\n\nThe precheck only answers \"can the company safely accept this package\"; it does not evaluate quality, and does not grade or rank plugins. Standards and checkers belong to the upstream toolchain, and their conclusions are author-provided evidence, not folded into the company precheck.\n\n## How to read the results\n\nThe precheck returns three conclusion fields that match the publish API's real behavior:\n\n| Conclusion field | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `can_submit` | No publish conflicts; the publish API will accept the package |\n| `will_publish` | No fail items under the open admission mode; published immediately on submit |\n| `requires_review` | Can be submitted, but goes into the pending-review queue |\n\nWhat really decides \"publish directly or enter review\" is the admission mode and fail items:\n\n| Case | Allowlist mode (default) | Open mode |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Publish conflict (package name / version taken / scope / ownership / same-name upstream package) | The publish API rejects | Same |\n| Has fail items (e.g. secrets, bundle structure errors) | Can be submitted, goes to pending review; **not a rejection** | Can be submitted, goes to pending review |\n| Only warnings, or all pass | Goes to pending review | No fail items; published immediately |\n\nA version number is taken as soon as submission succeeds; rejected or unpublished versions cannot be republished, so bump the version after fixing.\n\n## Pre-submit checklist\n\n1. Package after `pnpm build`, and make sure the `files` allowlist includes all build output.\n2. Run `dsh-primer check` and `npx dsh-standards@1.6.9 check .` locally, and keep the results in the author repo CI.\n3. The package must not contain `.tgz`, source maps, real secrets in test fixtures, or absolute local paths.\n4. Spell out `license`, `engines.dsh` and the DSH version line, so a mismatch does not surface only at install time.\n5. If install-time scripts are needed, explain in the submission notes what the scripts do.\n\n## FAQ\n\n| Symptom | Common cause | What to do |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Precheck reports a publish conflict | Package name not in the company scope, version already exists, same-name upstream package | These conflicts make the publish API reject outright; rename the package or bump the version first |\n| Precheck has fail items | bundle/patch structure errors, or suspected secrets detected | Fix before submitting; you can submit with fail items, but it goes to review and the version number is taken |\n| Goes to pending review after submission | Allowlist mode, or fail / warning items under open mode | Fix according to the review comments; do not keep retrying the same problem with a new version number |\n| Review takes a long time to conclude | Install scripts, dependencies or sensitive content need human confirmation | Add an explanation in the description, or contact an administrator |\n| Wrong version after installing inside the company | Client cache, or an old version was specified | Check the version in `dsh plugin add <package>@<version>` |\n\n## Full text in the repository\n\nThis page is only a guide; the full text on publishing, review and permissions lives in the repository docs (links point to the docs after merge):\n\n- [DSH plugin publishing (docs/dev/plugin-publish.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/dev/plugin-publish.md)\n- [Enterprise publishing, review and permissions (docs/enterprise-publishing.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/enterprise-publishing.md)\n- [Plugin author guide (docs/plugin-authors.md)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/plugin-authors.md)\n- [Mapping standards to admission thresholds (docs/plugin-quality.md)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/plugin-quality.md)\n- [Errors and FAQ (docs/dev/errors-faq.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/dev/errors-faq.md)\n",
  "quality-standards": "# Standards and checks: ENTRY / FLOW / BOOST\n\n## What each of the three layers answers\n\n\"Standards\" come from upstream dsh-plugin-guide / dsh-primer and dsh-standards. dsh-ee **does not run checkers built into the server**; repository docs may quote or adapt upstream text under their respective licenses — CC BY 4.0 requires keeping TASL, a Change Notice and a same-view disclaimer; Apache-2.0 requires keeping LICENSE/NOTICE. The site guides only write summaries and links, while the adapted text and per-document attribution live in the repository at [`docs/plugin-dev/standards/` (available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/plugin-dev/standards/README.md) and [`docs/dev/standards.md` (available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/dev/standards.md).\n\n| Layer | Question it answers | Who uses it | Where it sits in dsh-ee |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ENTRY | Whether a plugin's minimal shape holds (entry, manifest, license) | Authors, author repo CI | Aligned with the package-shape check in the publish precheck |\n| FLOW | Whether the plugin's flow holds up in real use (install, enable, uninstall, failure recovery) | Authors, testers | The example guides explain how to reproduce it |\n| BOOST | Whether experience and composability are in place (commands, rendering, skills, multiple surfaces) | Authors, reviewers | Examples are grouped by the surfaces involved |\n\n## How ENTRY / FLOW / BOOST are used on the company side\n\n| Use | Concrete practice | What not to do |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Author-provided evidence | Run the upstream checker (`dsh-standards@1.6.9`, requires Node ≥24.9) in the author repo CI, and pin the command and version | Do not treat upstream check results as the company's admission conclusion |\n| Company admission | After upload, read dsh-ee's own precheck details (pass / warn / fail and publish conflicts) | Do not use upstream checks in place of the precheck |\n| Review reference | During review, ask questions against the examples and standard clauses | Do not grade or rank plugins or examples on the page |\n| Learning path | Read the code from beginner to advanced following the examples | Do not treat examples as official recommendations |\n\n## What dsh-ee does not do\n\n- It does not run third-party checkers on the server, and does not provide a sandbox where checks can run.\n- It does not turn upstream check results into quality levels, scores or rankings.\n- It does not present adapted standards text as upstream original text: quoting or adapting must keep the source, the license (CC BY 4.0 / Apache-2.0) and a Change Notice, and adapted documents carry per-document attribution.\n- It does not promise \"officially recommended\": examples only show which kind of capability they demonstrate.\n\n## How authors leave evidence\n\n1. Run the pinned `dsh-standards@1.6.9` (Node ≥24.9) locally, and put the commands into the author repo CI.\n2. Save the JSON or HTML report as a build artifact, and record the generation time and host version.\n3. Reference the report location in the release notes instead of writing only a conclusion such as \"checked\".\n4. Rerun after an upstream standards upgrade; mark the old report with its version and do not mix them.\n\n```bash\n# 固定版本，避免 npx 同名包冲突\nnpx dsh-standards@1.6.9 check .\nnpx dsh-standards@1.6.9 check . --json > report.json\n```\n\nHow to read the checker's exit codes, report fields and \"not-run / NA / static does not mean pass\" is documented in the author reference in the repository (see the links below).\n\n## Relationship to the example pages\n\nThe example pages show \"one complete runnable minimal corridor\": each example states what you can learn, the prerequisites, the pinned version and the install command, and the detail page gives the source and license. Examples do not come with check conclusions, and the page does not rank examples by check results.\n\n## Full text in the repository\n\nThis page is only a guide; the standards text, clause index and checker usage live in the repository docs and upstream repositories (links point to the docs after merge):\n\n- [Standards and checks (docs/dev/standards.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/dev/standards.md)\n- [Standards and checker author reference (docs/plugin-dev/standards/README.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/plugin-dev/standards/README.md)\n- [Checker commands and report fields (docs/plugin-dev/standards/checker-cli.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/plugin-dev/standards/checker-cli.md)\n- [Evidence boundaries and common misreadings (docs/plugin-dev/standards/evidence-and-misreadings.md, available after merge)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/plugin-dev/standards/evidence-and-misreadings.md)\n- [Mapping standards to admission thresholds (docs/plugin-quality.md)](https://github.com/hikariming/dsh-ee/blob/main/docs/plugin-quality.md)\n- [Upstream: dsh-standards repository](https://github.com/PerryLink/dsh-standards) · [npm v1.6.9](https://www.npmjs.com/package/dsh-standards/v/1.6.9)\n- [Upstream: dsh-primer (DSH plugin development guide and toolchain)](https://github.com/PerryLink/dsh-primer)\n",
};
